{"answer":"No—not for an ordinary unpaid credit-card balance. In the United States, not paying a civil consumer debt like a credit card is not, by itself, a crime, and a private collection agency cannot have you arrested merely because you did not pay. Criminal issues can be different if there was actual fraud, identity theft, or a bad check situation, but your facts say this is just an ordinary unpaid credit-card balance, with no bounced check or fraud.

What the collector said is very likely illegal under the federal Fair Debt Collection Practices Act (FDCPA). A debt collector may not falsely represent or imply that nonpayment will result in arrest or imprisonment unless that is lawful and actually intended. They also may not threaten to take action they cannot legally take, or do not intend to take. Saying, "we will file theft charges and you will be arrested" over an ordinary unpaid credit-card debt is the kind of false criminal-threat collection tactic the FDCPA prohibits.

Save the voicemail, the text, phone numbers, dates/times, and any screenshots. You can report the collector to the CFPB and FTC, and you may want to speak with a consumer-rights attorney about an FDCPA claim. FDCPA lawsuits generally have a 1-year filing deadline from the violation, so do not wait if you want legal advice about suing.","abstain":false,"missing_fact":null,"citations":["15 U.S.C. § 1692a(5)","15 U.S.C. § 1692a(6)","15 U.S.C. § 1692d","15 U.S.C. § 1692e","15 U.S.C. § 1692e(4)","15 U.S.C. § 1692e(5)","15 U.S.C. § 1692e(7)","15 U.S.C. § 1692e(10)","15 U.S.C. § 1692k(d)","12 C.F.R. § 1006.18"]}

